    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "14 D. APPELO¨ AND Y. CHENG 4.2.2. Bratu problem. We use lrAA to solve the non-linear Bratu problem uxx + uyy + λeu = 0, (x,y) ∈ [0,1] × [0,1],"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 2,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.001, 631.236, 540.103, 672.152 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "with λ = 1 and homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions. To ﬁnd the approximate solution we discretize this equation using standard second order ﬁnite diﬀerence approximations for the x and y derivatives. Given an approximation X(i,j) ≈ u(xi,yj) this results in a function GB(i,j;X) describing the equation"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 250.56, 601.977, 260.832, 627.272 ],
    "font" : "CMMI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "1 h2x"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 264.24, 603.516, 423.624, 620.771 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "(X(i + 1,j) − 2X(i,j) + X(i − 1,j))"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 3,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 188.401, 609.903, 246.661, 620.552 ],
    "font" : "CMMI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "GB(i,j;X) ="
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 250.548, 615.348, 261.348, 615.828 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "2503.03909_pg14_images/imageFile1.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 596.79, 92.545, 606.752 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "(4.2)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 202.56, 575.577, 212.832, 600.872 ],
    "font" : "CMMI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "1 h2x"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 216.12, 576.996, 423.479, 595.991 ],
    "font" : "CMMI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "(X(i,j + 1) − 2X(i,j) + X(i,j − 1)) + λeX(i,j)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 193.56, 584.07, 201.301, 594.032 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "+"
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 202.548, 588.948, 213.348, 589.428 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "2503.03909_pg14_images/imageFile2.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 527.817, 540.206, 566.192 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Near the boundaries some of the terms in this expression will be set to zero to account for the homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions. In the numerical examples below we take the mesh to be xi = ihx, hx = m1+1 and yj = jhy, hy = n+11 with m = n = 200, making the setup the same as in [52]."
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 521.628, 548.988, 538.788, 549.468 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "2503.03909_pg14_images/imageFile3.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 160.068, 535.308, 175.188, 535.788 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "2503.03909_pg14_images/imageFile4.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "caption",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Caption",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 506.669, 539.98, 528.512 ],
    "linked content id" : 14,
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The ﬁxed point function G(i,j) is obtained by applying the preconditioned Richardson iteration. We have"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 438.629, 540.746, 505.39 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Xk+1(i,j) = G(i,j;Xk,α) ≡ Xk(i,j) + αM(GB(i,j;Xk)). We test lrAA with no preconditioner and with the ES preconditioner (corresponding to Rel1_x_n10.1E10) described above. The lrAA parameters used are the following TOL = 10−6,mˆ = 5,θ = 0.9,α = 0.125h2x (un-preconditioned case) and α = 0.1 (preconditioned case, no scheduling is used). In all experiments we use a rank 1 matrix with Frobenius norm around 1."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 17,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 378.87, 540.036, 436.592 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The results in Figure 11 display the numerical solutions obtained by lrAA methods with and without the ES preconditioner. In particular, both methods obtain visually similar numerical results in terms of solution contours and column and row index section for the ﬁnal iterates. The un-preconditioned lrAA gives a montonically increasing intermediate ranks. The ES-preconditioned lrAA converges very rapidly in 8 iterations."
  }, {
